#host("Farid Responds", ("Okay, so let's break this down. one of the top Shi narrators of all time, if not the top Shir narrator from Jaff, who is the most important Imam in terms of Sh the most quoted Shir im um is now uh cursing his student which makes him naturally unreliable. But Amar uses this report um in which Jafar says to the son of Zor, \"I only cursed your father to save his life so that they wouldn't kill him.\" Referring to the government. Now, there are multiple issues with this theory. The first is that Zor is uh having his reputation destroyed by Jaff.", "And it's not like Jaffer is saying this to one or two people. Jaffar wants this to be something that's wellknown that's established so that the uh califf at the time wouldn't uh assume that Zora is someone that's close to him. So this sort of cursing uh became common. Zur's son is now like curious what the hell is going on? Why is my father being cursed? Um this thing even spread to Sunni circles. Sunnis in their books have a narration that says that um Zor is from the hellfire and this is being said by so it's not like this um limited statement that only a couple people heard of.", "No, it's something that's become widespread and it has to become widespread because this is the imam's attempt to save Zur's life and to protect him allegedly based on this really strange theory. So the problem with this theory is that when the Imam curses Zur, he's disparaging his main source of information um that people would naturally go to in Iraq, Zor is in Iraq, uh people in Iraq, they're not going all the way down to Medina in order to seek knowledge. They're going to the local scholar there. They're going to Zor. However, when they become aware that the imam curses, there goes the thousands of narrations that they believed in or that they um are planning to apply when it comes to their or when it comes to their they're now in doubt of the information that they hear from Zor.", "And you see there's another issue with this excuse which is that if this was true if Jaffar was protecting his student so he started to curse him it has greater implications on Shi hadiths because you have other students that were also cursed by the imams and now we have to reject the clear obvious critiques by the imams and assume that the imam was probably just protecting them. And I'm not just speaking theoretically. We find practical examples of late Shir scholars rejecting the direct criticisms of the imams against these major Shia narrators. Oh no, no, the imam just said it as a form of to protect him in the same way he did to Zor. You see, you see how dangerous this uh excuse and this point of view and this theory is.",))
