#host("The Muslim Lantern", ("Not necessarily, no For example, I'll give you a very basic example There are Schools of Thought in Jurisprudence For example, there's a School of Thought called the Hanafi Jurisprudence الفقه الحنفي, for example. And they have a position on marriage When you conduct the marriage ceremony, is there a necessity for us to have two witnesses during the marriage ceremony? And the position of the Hanafi, for example, is you don't need two witnesses. (ERRATUM: See pinned comment for rectification) The overwhelming majority of the rest of the scholars say, no, you need witnesses Now, in this case, when you look at the Hanafi position, they don't have evidence saying that you don't need it, While the other side has clear cut evidence from the Prophet that there is no marriage without two witnesses And any marriage that was conducted without two witnesses is considered invalid So in this case, someone might ask, OK, why did they take that position to begin with?", "The reason is that the narration of the Prophet did not reach them. The information did not reach them So they based the ruling without the evidence reaching them, while we still have evidence from the other overwhelming majority of scholars So we do not take that position of that scholar because he does not have evidence to support this claim – Does that make sense now? I hope so",))
// T ML246 300,301,302,303 | Max | dash:300 | cut:0
